Category: API Testing & Automation
Topic: HTTP Methods, Status Codes & Postman Assertions
Question: Explain the core HTTP methods (GET, POST, PUT, PATCH, DELETE) and HTTP status code categories (2xx, 3xx, 4xx, 5xx). How do you write response assertions in Postman using pm.test?

Answer:
### 1. Conceptual Breakdown (Plain English)
**What it is:** GET retrieves data (idempotent); POST creates a new resource (non-idempotent); PUT replaces a full resource (idempotent); PATCH partially updates; DELETE removes a resource (idempotent).
**Why it matters in QA:** Understanding this concept enables QA engineers to bridge the gap between business specifications and technical verification, eliminating guesswork and test flakiness.

### 2. Deep-Dive Technical Explanation
GET retrieves data (idempotent); POST creates a new resource (non-idempotent); PUT replaces a full resource (idempotent); PATCH partially updates; DELETE removes a resource (idempotent). Status codes: 2xx success, 3xx redirection, 4xx client errors (401 unauthenticated vs 403 unauthorized), 5xx server errors. In Postman, test scripts execute in JavaScript using pm.test and pm.expect.

### 3. Step-by-Step Practical Application Guide
• **Step 1: Identify Preconditions & Scope:** Clarify the exact system boundary, inputs, and expected environmental states before executing.
• **Step 2: Construct Deterministic Assertions:** Avoid relying on arbitrary delays; instead, assert against explicit system state transitions and verifiable outcomes.
• **Step 3: Isolate Environmental Variables:** Ensure test data is cleanly provisioned and cleaned up so parallel or subsequent runs remain independent.

### 4. Key Terminology for QA Engineers
• **Deterministic Testing:** A testing approach where a given input always produces the exact same observable outcome without intermittent or random failures.
• **Flakiness (Flaky Test):** A test that intermittently passes and fails on identical application builds without any code changes, usually caused by timing race conditions or shared mutable state.
• **Quality Gate:** An automated checkpoint in the CI/CD pipeline that blocks flawed builds from progressing to staging or production.

Key Concepts Focus:
Idempotency (GET, PUT, DELETE are idempotent; POST is not). Status codes: 200 OK, 201 Created, 204 No Content, 400 Bad Request, 401 Unauthorized, 403 Forbidden, 404 Not Found, 500 Internal Server Error, 503 Service Unavailable. Postman pm.test syntax (pm.response.to.have.status, pm.response.json()).

Best Practice:
Chain requests in Postman by capturing tokens or IDs from response payloads and setting them into environment variables.

Common Pitfall to Avoid:
Only asserting on HTTP 200 status code without verifying JSON schema or response body field values.

Code Example:
```
// Postman Tests Script:
pm.test("Status is 201 Created", function () {
  pm.response.to.have.status(201);
});
pm.test("Response contains order ID and status", function () {
  const json = pm.response.json();
  pm.expect(json.status).to.eql("PENDING");
  pm.expect(json.orderId).to.be.a("string");
  pm.environment.set("currentOrderId", json.orderId);
});
```

